<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Muti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">914–<a href="/years/974">974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Muti li-llah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Muti was the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 946 to 974, placed on the throne by the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> after the blinding and deposition of al-Mustakfi. Throughout his reign real power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> lay with the Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and the caliph was reduced to a figurehead who conferred titles and legitimacy. Paralysed by a stroke, he abdicated in 974 in favour of his son al-Ta'i.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-muti.jpg" alt="al-Muti" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MansurISamanidCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Muti was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 913/14 as al-Fadl, a son of the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir and a Slavic concubine, Mash'ala. He was the brother of the caliphs al-Radi and al-Muttaqi and grew up in a time of crisis, as military strongmen took control of the provinces and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> itself. The commander-in-chief Tuzun deposed and blinded al-Muttaqi in September 944 and chose al-Mustakfi, from a rival line, as caliph. The two princes were said to hate each other, and al-Fadl, who like his father was known for his piety, went into hiding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In December 945 the Daylamite troops of the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Al-Mustakfi was deposed on 29 January 946, or 9 March according to other accounts, and Mu'izz al-Dawla raised al-Fadl to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> that day as al-Muti li-llah. The deposed caliph was blinded, apparently at al-Muti's instigation, and died a prisoner in September 949.</p><p>Al-Muti was in effect a puppet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> and then of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>. The Buyids still acted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s name and sought from him the honorific titles and diplomas that gave them legitimacy, while his own duties shrank to oversight of the judiciary, religious institutions and the Abbasid clan. A daily salary of 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>, later supplemented by estates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, paid for gifts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, support for needy relatives and several pavilions in the palace grounds.</p><p>Outside Buyid lands his authority declined. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> refused to recognise him until a peace in 955, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 969 and began to expand into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. In 972, when Byzantine raids reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> and Edessa, refugees crowded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a> pressed al-Muti to sell his valuables and provide 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>. He protested in a much-quoted letter but complied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 1 August 974 the Turkic commander Sabuktakin seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Al-Muti, aged and paralysed on his right side after a stroke in 970, was induced to abdicate on 5 August in favour of his son Abd al-Karim, who became al-Ta'i. It was the first succession from father to son since 902. Al-Muti died on 12 October 974 at Dayr al-Aqul on a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>. Medieval and modern writers regard his reign as the lowest point of Abbasid power, though it brought stability: it lasted 29 Hijri years and four months, far longer than those of his predecessors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Muti'” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliph al-Mustakfi gave the brothers the honorific titles by which they are known, Imad al-Dawla, Rukn al-Dawla and Mu'izz al-Dawla, 'support', 'pillar' and 'strengthener' of the state; a few weeks later Mu'izz al-Dawla had him blinded and deposed and installed <b>al-Muti</b> in his place.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Turkic commander Sabuktakin seized Baghdad from the Buyid Izz al-Dawla, the aged and paralysed caliph <b>al-Muti</b>' was induced to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=953" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of al-Muti"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6016 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6016 4653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=953">What was here in 953 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
